  <p><strong>Dwell time optimisation:</strong> The dwell time — the technical duration required for these chemical reactions to complete at the surface — is a critical formulation design parameter. An aerosol formulator must ensure that the evaporation rate of the spray maintains a wet film on the target surface for the full EPA or CDSCO mandated contact duration, typically 2 to 10 minutes depending on the active and target pathogen.</p>
</section>

<!-- 3. ENGINEERING FOR COVERAGE: PARTICLE SIZE AND DISTRIBUTION -->
<section class="section" id="3-engineering-for-coverage-particle-size-and-distribution">
  <h2>3. Engineering for Coverage: Particle Size and Distribution</h2>
  <p>Aerosolised disinfectants must be engineered to deliver a specific droplet size range to maximise surface contact while minimising inhalation and runoff risks. The optimal droplet size for surface-acting disinfectant aerosols is typically between 40 and 80 microns. Droplets smaller than approximately 10 microns remain airborne as dry fog, failing to wet surfaces adequately and posing inhalation hazards. Droplets larger than 150 microns coalesce on impact, creating coalescence pooling on horizontal surfaces and runoff on vertical ones — wasting the active concentrate and potentially damaging sensitive electronic or polished surfaces in healthcare settings.</p>
  <p>Droplet size is determined by the interaction between the formulation viscosity, propellant vapour pressure, and actuator orifice geometry. The aerosol's <a href="/aerosol-particle-size-distribution-measurement/" target="_top">particle size distribution measurement</a> — using laser diffraction or cascade impactor methods — is a key quality attribute that must be controlled during product development and validated during manufacturing. The engineering of surface coverage is therefore a physics and fluid dynamics problem as much as a chemistry one.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Surface tension reduction:</strong> Professional formulations incorporate non-ionic surfactants to reduce contact angle on surfaces, allowing the droplet to spread into microscopic pits and textural features of the substrate.</li>
    <li><strong>Pressure and valve dynamics:</strong> The internal can pressure, combined with the valve's vapour tap ratio, dictates the degree of mechanical break-up during the liquid-to-spray transition at the actuator orifice.</li>
    <li><strong>Actuator selection:</strong> The final spray pattern — whether a fine mist, wide cone, or directed stream — is determined by the internal geometry of the actuator's swirl chamber and orifice diameter. For a detailed treatment, see our guide on <a href="/aerosol-actuators-101/" target="_top">aerosol actuator design and spray patterns</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

      <!-- 4. NAVIGATING THE REGULATORY LANDSCAPE (GLOBAL & INDIA) -->
      <section class="section" id="4-navigating-the-regulatory-landscape-global-india">
        <h2>4. Navigating the Regulatory Landscape (Global &amp; India)</h2>
        <p>Aerosols designed for disinfection are classified as biocides or pesticide products, subjecting them to strict regulatory control globally and in India. Unlike conventional cleaning products, disinfectant aerosols must demonstrate quantified antimicrobial efficacy through standardised third-party testing before any antimicrobial claim can be made on the label or in marketing. In the US, the <strong>Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)</strong> manages disinfectant registrations under FIFRA, accessible through the <a href="https://www.epa.gov/antimicrobial-pesticides" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EPA antimicrobial pesticides</a> programme. In India, the <strong>CDSCO (Central Drugs Standard Control Organization)</strong> governs these products under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, specifically under Schedule O for disinfectants.</p>
        <p>Regulatory requirements also extend to the aerosol container itself — pressure vessel compliance under DOT, UN, and regional regulations applies regardless of the nature of the contents. Navigating these overlapping compliance frameworks early prevents costly reformulation or product recall after launch. Our guide on <a href="/dot-un-aerosol-transport-regulations-hazmat/" target="_top">DOT and UN aerosol transport regulations</a> covers the hazmat classification, Limited Quantity exemptions, and air transport rules that apply to disinfectant aerosols in the supply chain.</p>
            
        <h3>4.1 Indian Compliance: CDSCO and Schedule 'O'</h3>
        <p>Manufacturers targeting the Indian market must demonstrate compliance with Schedule 'O' standards, which specify testing against defined bacterial strains to minimum log-reduction benchmarks. The technical dossier for CDSCO registration must include stability data demonstrating active potency at end of shelf life and AOAC efficacy data. The Indian regulatory framework also requires product labelling to comply with the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, including declaration of active ingredients and their concentrations.</p>

        <h3>4.2 EPA List N and Global Standards</h3>
        <p>Products intended for healthcare use in the US must be registered on specialised lists such as EPA List N for SARS-CoV-2 efficacy. This requires testing against designated harder-to-kill pathogen surrogates according to the <strong>AOAC 961.02 Germicidal Spray Test</strong> protocol, which proves 100% kill across specified replicates under defined conditions. EU-market products must additionally comply with the Biocidal Products Regulation (BPR) administered by the <a href="https://www.echa.europa.eu/regulations/biocidal-products-regulation/understanding-bpr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ECHA</a>.</p>

